{"name":"Monarch Wing","hex":"#ff8d25","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"monarch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The ruler of an absolute monarchy or the head of state of a constitutional monarchy.","A monarch butterfly (Danaus plexippus) and others of genus Danaus, found primarily in North America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/monarch"},{"term":"wing","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An appendage of an animal's (bird, bat, insect) body that enables it to fly.","A fin at the side of a ray or similar fish."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To injure slightly (as with a gunshot), especially in the wing or arm.","To fly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wing"}],"entries":[{"title":"Monarch","blocks":[{"text":"A monarch is a head of state for life or until abdication, and therefore the head of state of a monarchy. A monarch usually exercises the highest authority and power in the state. Usually, a monarch either personally inherits the lawful right to exercise the state's sovereign rights or is selected by an established process from a family or cohort eligible to provide the nation's monarch. Alternatively, an individual may proclaim oneself monarch, or even usurp power, as many Ancient Greek tyrants did. If a young child is crowned the monarch, then a regent is often appointed to govern until the monarch reaches the requisite adult age to rule."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarch","wikidata":"Q116"},{"title":"Wing","blocks":[{"text":"A wing is a structure which produces both lift and drag while moving through air. Wings are defined by two shape characteristics, an airfoil section and a planform. Wing efficiency is expressed as lift-to-drag ratio, which compares the benefit of lift with the air resistance of a given wing shape, as it flies. Aerodynamics includes the study of wing performance in air."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg/330px-Wing.two.arp.600pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wing","wikidata":"Q161358"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG/500px-Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG/1280px-Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG/500px-Cloud_over_A340_wing.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Condensation in the low-pressure region over the wing of an Airbus A340, passing through humid air","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACloud_over_A340_wing.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg/500px-Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg/1280px-Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg/500px-Bmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The wing of a landing BMI Airbus A319-100. The slats at its leading edge and the flaps at its trailing edge are extended.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABmi_a319-100_g-dbca_closeup_arp.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/PSM_V19_D181_Various_seeds_of_trees.jpg/120px-PSM_V19_D181_Various_seeds_of_trees.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca